A maintainable Socket.IO integration should separate the socket connection from individual React components and make event subscription and cleanup predictable.

The biggest mistakes usually come from accidentally creating multiple connections or registering the same event listener repeatedly.

### Create the Socket in a Dedicated Module

```javascript
// socket.js
import { io } from "socket.io-client";

export const socket = io(
  "https://api.example.com",
  {
    autoConnect: false
  }
);
```

This creates one shared socket instance instead of creating a new connection every time a component renders.

### Connect from an Appropriate Application Boundary

For example, a provider can manage the connection:

```jsx
function SocketProvider({ children }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    socket.connect();

    return () => {
      socket.disconnect();
    };
  }, []);

  return children;
}
```

In a real application, connection lifetime may also depend on authentication. For example, you may connect only after the current user is known.

### Subscribe to Events in Components

```jsx
function Notifications() {
  const [notifications, setNotifications] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    function handleNotification(notification) {
      setNotifications((notifications) => [
        notification,
        ...notifications
      ]);
    }

    socket.on(
      "notification:new",
      handleNotification
    );

    return () => {
      socket.off(
        "notification:new",
        handleNotification
      );
    };
  }, []);

  // ...
}
```

### Why Cleanup Is Important

Suppose the component mounts three times and never removes its listener.

One server event could trigger:

```text
notification:new
      ↓
handler 1
handler 2
handler 3
```

The UI might then display the same notification three times.

Correct cleanup keeps the subscription lifecycle predictable:

```text
Component mounts
→ socket.on(...)

Component unmounts
→ socket.off(...)
```

### Remove the Same Handler You Added

This structure is clear:

```jsx
function handleMessage(message) {
  // ...
}

socket.on("message:new", handleMessage);

return () => {
  socket.off("message:new", handleMessage);
};
```

Avoid creating unrelated anonymous functions for registration and cleanup because they are different function references.

### Avoid Registering Application Events Inside connect

A tempting pattern is:

```javascript
socket.on("connect", () => {
  socket.on("message:new", handleMessage);
});
```

The socket may reconnect multiple times, causing another `message:new` handler to be added after each reconnection.

Prefer registering normal application event handlers independently:

```javascript
socket.on("connect", handleConnect);
socket.on("message:new", handleMessage);
```

### Use a Provider or Custom Hook for Shared Connection State

The application may expose:

```text
connected
disconnected
socket
```

through Context:

```jsx
const SocketContext = createContext(null);
```

Then a custom Hook can provide access:

```jsx
function useSocket() {
  return useContext(SocketContext);
}
```

This is useful when many features need the same connection.

### Keep Connection State Separate from Application Data

A socket connection might expose:

```text
connected = true
```

while application data includes:

```text
messages
notifications
online users
```

Do not put every piece of real-time application data inside one socket object or provider simply because it arrives through the same connection.

Store data where its feature naturally owns it.

### Authentication

A Socket.IO client can send authentication information during connection according to the server architecture.

Conceptually:

```text
React knows authenticated session/user
        ↓
Socket establishes authenticated connection
        ↓
Server verifies identity
        ↓
Server joins appropriate user/feature channels
```

The server must authenticate the socket independently rather than trusting a user ID sent by the client.

